  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nStratus is lingering over the Northland early this morning in \nthe wake of yesterday's rainfall. Where the stratus has cleared,\nwe should see some areas of fog, with some locations already \nexperiencing visibilities of a mile or less. Since there is a \nlarge area of cleared stratus from the I-35 corridor east \nthrough northwest Wisconsin, a Dense Fog Advisory may be needed \nin this area. This area did not receive much rainfall over the \nlast 24 hours, so the fog is likely to be more patchy in nature.\n\n\nUpper-level ridging builds over the area today, which should \nhelp provide a dry day today. Expect partly to mostly sunny \nskies, with clouds diminishing through the day. near to slightly\nabove normal high temperatures to range from the mid 60s to the\nlower 70s. Tonight, quiet conditions continue with light winds,\nwhich could once again allow for fog to develop during the \novernight hours.\n\nA fast-moving upper level shortwave will approach the region \nfrom the west on Saturday, riding over the top of the upper \nlevel ridging. This will bring increasing clouds and a chance \nfor rain showers, primarily during the afternoon and night time \nhours. The highest chances for measurable rainfall will be \nfocused over central and north-central Minnesota, though amounts\nwill be light and generally remain under a tenth of an inch. \nHigh temperatures on Saturday will be slightly cooler. Highs \nwill range from the low 60s under the cloud cover to the upper \n60s and low 70s elsewhere. The showers will linger into Saturday\nnight before tapering off early Sunday morning. Clouds will \nthen gradually diminish through the day on Sunday, with \notherwise dry and mild conditions. Once again, high temperatures\nshould be in the 60s. \n\nFor the last couple days we have been eyeing a much more \ndynamic and large scale weather system expected to impact the \nUpper Midwest early next week. By Monday, southeasterly winds \nwill increase as a robust area of low pressure begins to take \nshape over the Plains. The upper level trough associated with \nthis system has been depicted on the ensemble models as moving \nacross the Rockies Monday and Tuesday, then ejecting out across \nthe plains Wednesday. The energy with this is likely to be split\nbetween a northern stream and a southern by the time it gets to\nus, which will affect the timing and precipitation amounts we \nwill receive. Most of the models bring a widespread, soaking \nrainfall across much of the Northland beginning Monday night and\ncontinuing through Tuesday night. Anomalously high moisture \nsurging northward ahead of this system may have a tropical \nconnection, with moisture from the remnants of Polo or Odalys \nstreaming in our direction. This may lead to much needed \nrainfall totals, with a few rumbles of thunder possible Monday \nnight. Showers will gradually diminish on Wednesday as the \nsystem departs to the east, ushering in cooler and drier air. \nHigh temperatures will drop into the lower and middle 60s for \nthe middle and latter half of next week.",
